<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Copenhagen (1801)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> was fought on 2 April 1801 when a British fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyde-parker">Sir Hyde Parker</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> leading the attack, engaged the Danish-Norwegian fleet moored before Copenhagen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> struck because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-armed-neutrality">League of Armed Neutrality</a>. After hard fighting a truce was agreed, and Denmark left the league. Nelson is said to have ignored a signal to withdraw.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-copenhagen-1801.jpg" alt="Battle of Copenhagen (1801)" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicholas Pocock · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Copenhagen,_2_April_1801_RMG_BHC0529.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1801, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s main advantage over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was its navy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> searched and seized neutral ships trading with French ports. The Russian tsar Paul, once a British ally, formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-armed-neutrality">League of Armed Neutrality</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to enforce free trade with France. The British saw it as a threat to their supply of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and naval stores from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. They also feared that, once the ice melted, the Russian fleet would join the Danish and Swedish fleets, together up to 123 ships of the line.</p><p>A fleet under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyde-parker">Sir Hyde Parker</a>, with Vice-Admiral Horatio Nelson as second-in-command, sailed from Yarmouth on 12 March 1801 and learned at the Skaw on 19 March that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> had rejected an ultimatum. Parker was cautious, and Nelson persuaded him to attack the Danish fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> had used the delay to prepare. Old hulks and ships not fitted for sea were moored along the shore of Amager as floating batteries, and the Tre Kroner fort at the northern end had 68 guns. Parker gave Nelson twelve ships of the line with the shallowest drafts and the smaller vessels. On 2 April three British ships ran aground, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>, Russell and Bellona, which weakened the northern end of the line. The Danish batteries opened fire at 10:05 am, and by 11:30 the battle was general.</p><p>At 1:30 pm Parker, who could see only distress signals through the smoke, hoisted the signal of recall. Nelson, who is said to have put his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> to his blind eye, did not obey; only Captain Edward Riou withdrew and was killed. By about 2:00 pm much of the Danish line had fallen silent. Nelson then sent a note under a flag of truce to Crown Prince Frederik, and a ceasefire was agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Of the 18 Danish ships engaged, two had sunk, one had exploded and twelve were captured. The Danish flagship Dannebroge blew up at 4:30 pm, killing 250 men. British losses were returned as 274 killed and 737 wounded; Danish casualties are uncertain, Olfert Fischer estimating at least 1,600 to 1,800 killed or wounded. The next day Nelson secured an armistice, and the news of Tsar Paul's assassination, which reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> before the British, led to the League's dissolution. A final peace was signed on 23 October 1801, and Parker was recalled in May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Copenhagen (1801)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Denmark joined the League of Armed Neutrality, a British fleet under Hyde Parker and Horatio Nelson defeated the Danish fleet at the <b>Battle of Copenhagen</b> in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The navy remained strong until the British attacked it at the <b>Battle of Copenhagen</b> in 1801 and seized it in 1807.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li></ul></section>
